#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.9"
# dependencies = ["pyyaml"]
# ///
"""tools/promote — promote lectures from ``mirror/`` into the ``lectures/`` pool.

Promotion copies a lecture from ``mirror/<series>/lectures/`` into the flat
pool at ``lectures/``, together with its ASSET CLOSURE — the files the lecture
actually references (figures, includes, ``:load:`` scripts, downloads), found
by parsing the MyST source. Nothing is copied wholesale: only referenced
assets enter the pool, so orphans are excluded by construction.

Dedup happens at promotion time: the same-named copy is hashed across every
non-excluded series in ``sync/manifest.yml``; the pool takes the copy from the
CANONICAL series (the highest-priority series, in manifest order, whose copy is
identical to the requested source) and records every identical series as a
source. "Identical" means byte-identical, or identical once each copy's asset
references are normalised to the pool paths promotion would give them.

A same-named copy that DIFFERS is treated as a different lecture under the same
name, and promotion refuses it: the error names the lecture and every series
involved. A copy that is merely STALE is not different: a series recorded as a
source at the entry's last promotion whose copy is still at a version recorded
then (its own, or the canonical copy's), while the canonical copy moved on,
stays a source (the drift-check's ``stale-copy``, which is info). Only a copy
edited to something else is refused, and a canonical-map entry that names a
stale copy, since following it would take the pool back to the lagging text.
Which copy the pool keeps never falls out of run order. The one exception is
the committed canonical map, ``sync/canonical.yml`` (human intent), which
names the winning series per slug; the losing copies are then recorded under
``divergent:`` and the entry is marked ``interim: true`` until a rename or
merge upstream retires the map entry.

Series classes (``class:`` on a manifest stanza). The default class is
canonical-eligible. A ``consumer`` series (one that republishes other series'
lectures) is never canonical and contributes no lecture to the pool; its
same-named copies, equal or not, are recorded under ``superseded:`` and are
NOT watched by the drift-check. No series is excluded by default, and CI
excludes none now that ``jax`` is promoted. ``--exclude`` drops series for one
run.

Asset references are normalised into the pool ``_static`` conventions
(``_static/<slug>/`` for per-lecture assets, ``_static/_shared/`` for assets
used by two or more lectures) via an ORDERED LIST OF LITERAL STRING
REPLACEMENTS, each recorded in ``sync/ledger.yml``. Applying exactly those
replacements to the canonical mirror copy reproduces the pool copy
byte-for-byte — the invariant the drift-check relies on. No unrecorded
transformation of lecture text is permitted.

Two files record the outcome:

* ``sync/ledger.yml`` — MACHINE STATE: provenance, pins, assets, and rewrites
  per pool lecture (written by this script; do not hand-edit).
* ``lectures/``       — the pool itself: ``<slug>.md`` files plus ``_static/``.

Usage
-----
    tools/promote SLUG [SLUG...]   # promote named lectures (source resolved
                                   # per-lecture, highest-priority series wins)
    tools/promote --toc dp         # promote every lecture in dp's _toc.yml
    tools/promote --refresh        # re-promote entries whose canonical pin moved
    tools/promote --retire SLUG [SLUG...]   # take lectures out of the pool

Retirement. A lecture renamed or removed upstream leaves the pool by being
RETIRED: its ledger entry and pool file go, and so do the assets no other
pool lecture uses. ``--toc SERIES`` retires on its own the entries canonical
in SERIES whose lecture has left SERIES' toc, whose canonical copy is gone,
and whose name no series in the manifest holds any more (at most RETIRE_CAP
in one run: more than that retires none and fails, since a toc emptied by
mistake must not empty the pool). Anything else is retired by name with
``--retire``. ``--refresh`` never retires: it skips an entry whose canonical
copy is gone, so the rest still refresh. Retirement only ever acts on an
entry whose canonical series is in ``sync/manifest.yml``: a series the pool
has taken over (cut over) is out of the manifest, so nothing of it is retired
by these tools. Git history and the run's summary are the record of a
retirement; the ledger keeps none.

A file lectures share. The pool keeps ONE copy of an asset, used by every
pool lecture that refers to it, while the series those lectures come from
each carry their own (the ``_admonition/gpu.md`` include, a ``.py`` file two
lectures load, a figure a lecture and its variant show). The asset's ledger
record lists those series' copies as its ``sources``: only the series its
user lectures come from, never one that merely has a file of that name.

* When a series whose copy was the pool's version changes it, the pool takes
  the new version for every lecture that uses the file, in the batch or not,
  and the copies that did not follow are recorded as STALE on the asset
  (their own ``digest`` and a ``stale_since`` time) until they catch up. The
  drift-check reports a stale copy, as information at first and as drift
  once it is a week old. A copy that was never the pool's version (it
  differs when first seen) is a different file under the same name: it is
  recorded with ``differs: true``, reported as information, and never
  followed or given to a lecture.
* Two series that change the file differently, and a stale copy that changes
  to anything but the pool's version, are refused.
* Lectures that share a file stay on one pool path. A lecture outside the
  batch counts as a user of the version the batch brings, so a shared file is
  never split in two; and when a file's pool path must change (a second
  lecture starts to use it), the lectures that use it are pulled into the
  batch and re-promoted with it. One that cannot be holds the mover back.
* Two lectures that bring different bytes to one new pool path are both
  refused, naming both: which file the pool keeps never falls out of run
  order.

Flags
-----
    --toc SERIES       take the slug list from mirror/SERIES/lectures/_toc.yml
                       (product pages — about, intro, status, troubleshooting,
                       zreferences — are skipped: they become products/<key>/
                       pages in M3, not pool content). For a consumer series
                       each lecture comes from its canonical home instead.
    --refresh          re-run the promotion pipeline for every ledger entry
                       whose canonical series pin in sync/state.yml differs
                       from its recorded promoted_at pin; unchanged pins are a
                       strict no-op. An entry whose canonical series this run
                       cannot use (excluded, or a consumer) is skipped while
                       its pin is unchanged and FAILS once the pin has moved.
                       An entry whose canonical copy is gone is skipped, never
                       retired.
                       CI runs it with --exclude $PROMOTE_EXCLUDE (see
                       .github/workflows/drift-check.yml).
    --retire           with SLUG...: retire the named pool lectures. Refused
                       for a lecture its canonical series' toc still lists
                       (or, with no toc in the mirror, whose canonical copy
                       still exists), for a lecture another canonical-eligible
                       series still holds and lists, and for an entry whose canonical series
                       is not in the manifest or is excluded from the run.
                       All or nothing: one refusal retires none.
    --exclude SERIES [SERIES...]
                       series excluded from source/dedup consideration for
                       this run (none is excluded by default).
    --dry-run          print the full plan (sources, closures, rewrites,
                       collisions, skips) without writing anything.
    -h/--help          show help and exit.

This script NEVER commits anything and NEVER touches ``mirror/`` — it only
reads the mirror and writes the pool plus ``sync/ledger.yml``.
"""

